##  What I Learned

### Iterable

An iterable is an object that can be traversed one element at a time.

Examples:

```python
numbers = [10, 20, 30]
name = "MANOJ"
```

### Iterator

An iterator is an object that provides values one at a time using `next()`.

```python
numbers = [10, 20, 30]

iterator = iter(numbers)

print(next(iterator))
print(next(iterator))
print(next(iterator))
```

Output:

```text
10
20
30
```

### StopIteration

When an iterator has no more elements, calling `next()` raises `StopIteration`.

I practiced handling it using `try-except`:

```python
numbers = [10, 20]

iterator = iter(numbers)

try:
    print(next(iterator))
    print(next(iterator))
    print(next(iterator))
except StopIteration:
    print("No more elements")
```

Output:

```text
10
20
No more elements
```

